cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you cite this protocol or the accompanying code, please use the metadata below."
title: "Three gates, three proxies: auditing the null-report checks of a pre-registered LLM-agent evaluation"
# This abstract must match the Abstract section of manuscript/main.md verbatim.
# analysis/scripts/check_claim_boundary.py compares them on every run, so editing
# one without the other fails repro.sh.
abstract: >-
  We audit the three gates by which one of our own pre-registered evaluations fixed what a null
  result would be worth. Given one fixed prompt set in a simulated world of five named zones, a
  language-model agent names its next zone. The evaluation asked whether a channel from the agent's
  recent movement to its sampling shifts that choice by more than a total-variation margin of 0.10.
  On the records, each condition was sampled at one fixed temperature and top-p, and each channel-on
  block ran first. Each gate read a quantity other than the one its reading depends on. The power
  gate reads a chi-square surrogate. A post hoc simulation fixed before it ran, shifting the first
  run's channel-off distribution along the surrogate's direction, puts the permutation test that
  decides at the surrogate's 1.0 from half the margin upward, but at a tenth of the margin the
  surrogate returns 1.0 where the test rejects in 122 of 1,000 simulated replicates. The entropy
  floor decided the outcome: it stopped the rules on the second of two models although each cell
  produced two to four zones, and passed two runs whose channel-off draws leave two of the five
  zones empty. The cap on draws with no recorded zone passed every run, reading the worst cell
  rather than the difference between conditions. A test specified after that difference was seen, on
  draws already collected, found such draws 2.6 and 1.9 percentage points more often in both arms'
  channel-on blocks (156 against 94; 107 against 61), assuming exchangeable draws, mostly the string
  "null" in one arm and missing or malformed JSON in the other, confounded with execution order.
  This case motivates three diagnostics: simulate the power of the test that decides, report support
  beside entropy, and compare dropped outputs between conditions. The claims concern these gates on
  two models and one prompt, not the effect of the channel.
type: software
